{
  "dom_id": "step-q344884",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/338c4ecb15136af6f7dca3eb9cf01e396ba1df7f64525bebeaa435cb800b618b.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{\u2032}(-2)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)-3g(x).$\n\n${h}^{\u2032}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(x)=2{f}^{\u2032}(x)-3{g}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(-2)=2{f}^{\u2032}(-2)-3{g}^{\u2032}(-2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-3,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-3,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/97303b0f041d638b25388c322828f59bb335a8bf20a7641a62edcbae87490c64.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-3,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u0394f}{\u0394x}=\\frac{2-(-1)}{0-(-3)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(-2)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-3,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u0394g}{\u0394x}=\\frac{0-3}{0-(-3)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{\u2032}(-2)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(-2) & =2{f}^{\u2032}(-2)-3{g}^{\u2032}(-2) \\\\  & =2(1)-3(-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
